<svg class="diagram-svg" viewBox="0 0 740 296" role="img" aria-labelledby="bdl31priormatching-title bdl31priormatching-desc" data-pagefind-ignore>
  <title id="bdl31priormatching-title">Schematic of imposing Gaussian process priors on Bayesian neural networks by Wasserstein distance minimization</title>
  <desc id="bdl31priormatching-desc">Left: a Gaussian process with kernel k(x, x′) supplies samples f_gp drawn from GP(0, K); a (Bayesian) neural network with parameters theta drawn from p(theta | psi) supplies samples f_nn obtained by integrating p(f | theta) over p(theta). Both sample streams enter a central loop. Its upper box is a Lipschitz network g that computes the supremum over g of the expectation of g(f_nn) under p_nn minus the expectation of g(f_gp) under p_gp, repeated in an inner loop; it passes the optimum g* down to a lower box that evaluates W1 as the difference of the expectations of g* and takes the infimum over psi of W1(p_nn(f_nn; psi), p_gp(f_gp)), repeated in an outer loop. The resulting psi* is passed to the right, to a (Bayesian) neural network with optimized priors p(theta | psi*).</desc>
